A transport, a *netlayer*, moves a session's bytes between two peers. The core
hands each frame to whichever netlayer a
[locator]($link.page('concepts'))'s transport symbol names, and never learns which
one carried it. The transports below ship today. A peer that cannot open a socket
of its own, like a browser, reaches a socket transport through
[the relay]($link.page('relay')), or holds a direct WebRTC DataChannel to another
browser with no socket at all. If you are not sure which to reach for,
[Choosing a transport]($link.page('transports/choosing')) walks the decision.

> **Netlayer**: pancake's word for a transport, a resident you register with the
> driver, met by a contract (produce a connection over a stream) rather than a
> privileged built-in.

## A transport is a resident, not a privilege

The driver holds a slice of netlayers and dispatches `connectVia` and
`acceptVia` on the transport symbol it reads off a locator. Every resident funnels
through one transport-generic `Connection` (a reader, a writer, and a close handle),
so it reuses the one framing core instead of reinventing it. A new transport is one
new resident and a registry entry; the driver and the core do not change to admit
it.

| Transport | Symbol | Channel | Addressed by | Reach for it when |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| TCP | `tcp` | cleartext TCP, no TLS | `host:port` | a real network, or interop with Goblins over TCP |
| Keyed TCP | `keyed-tcp` | cleartext TCP, key-derived designator | Ed25519 pubkey hex; socket address in `direct-addresses` | restart-stable designators and key-squatter refusal ([ADR-0075](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0075-keyed-tcp-designator.md)) |
| In-process | `inproc` | in-memory byte pipes, no sockets | a name within one process | tests and demos that run both peers in one process |
| iroh | `iroh` | encrypted QUIC (separate artifact) | a self-authenticating node id in an `ocapn://` URI | you want an encrypted channel today, or peer-to-peer reach |
| WebRTC | `webrtc` | encrypted DataChannel over DTLS (separate artifact) | an Ed25519 designator reached through a signaling capability, not a dialable address | two browsers speaking directly, or a peer-to-peer channel a NAT would otherwise block |

### TCP

The default real-network transport, and the one that addresses a Goblins peer. It
is cleartext, no TLS, because `tcp` has to speak the same wire as Goblins' cleartext
`tcp`. The transport is proven against a live
[Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) vat over a real socket, then
re-exposed over the C ABI as blocking endpoint handles. Two I/O paths sit above it:
a blocking pump for cooperative request/response, and the completion loop (io_uring
on Linux, kqueue on macOS and BSD) for many connections without a thread apiece.

### Keyed TCP

Same cleartext TCP sockets as `tcp`, different locator grammar. The designator is
the listener's Ed25519 public key in canonical hex, and the socket address rides the
`direct-addresses` hint. The driver pins the handshake to the dialed key so a
partner can re-dial the same designator across a process restart, and a key
squatter is refused before the session opens
([ADR-0075](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0075-keyed-tcp-designator.md)).
Bindings expose `listen_keyed_tcp` / `KeyedTcpServer` and `keyed_tcp_locator`.

### In-process (inproc)

No sockets at all. A switchboard wires a dialer and a listener through a pair of
in-memory byte pipes, one per direction, so a full OCapN conversation runs inside a
single process. It exists to prove the netlayer contract is not accidentally
socket-shaped, and it is what the examples and tests use to drive both halves of a
conversation without binding a port.

### iroh

An encrypted, peer-to-peer transport:
[QUIC](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9000.html) carried by an
[iroh](https://www.iroh.computer/) Rust sidecar staticlib, kept in a separate
optional library, `libpancake_net_transport_iroh`, so the base archives stay pure
Zig. A peer is reached by its self-authenticating Ed25519 endpoint id in an
`ocapn://<node-id>.iroh` URI rather than a `host:port`. That one key is at once the
network route, the OCapN designator, and the session signing key. A dial resolves
three ways: by direct-address hints, by a deterministic local address book, and by
node id alone over the public n0 relays and DNS/pkarr discovery. A loop host can
serve many connections from one thread. The dedicated
[iroh page]($link.page('transports/iroh')) covers all of it.

### WebRTC

A peer-to-peer transport for the browser and across NATs: a
[WebRTC](https://www.w3.org/TR/webrtc/) DataChannel carried by a Rust sidecar
staticlib on the native side and `RTCPeerConnection` in the browser, kept in a
separate optional library, `libpancake_net_transport_webrtc`, so the base
archives stay pure Zig. Unlike the other transports, a WebRTC peer has no
dialable address. The two ends first exchange an offer, an answer, and ICE
candidates through a signaling capability that both can reach, which is a room, a
relay, or any OCapN object. The channel then opens directly between them, and the
keyed handshake runs over it with the signaling host out of the byte path. The
DataChannel is DTLS-encrypted, and the designator is pinned by the keyed
handshake the same way keyed-tcp and iroh pin theirs. `@pancake/webrtc` drives
the browser half: two browsers introduced by a room speak directly with the room
out of the message path, and a native peer meets a browser the same way. A
three-party handoff redeems over a WebRTC give by signaling-then-handshake, so a
gifted capability is reached over a channel that never had an address. The
dedicated [WebRTC page]($link.page('transports/webrtc')) covers the signaling
exchange, the browser arm, and the handoff.

## Encryption is the netlayer's job

Confidentiality is something a netlayer provides, not a flag the API sets. The
contract a transport meets says nothing about whether the stream is encrypted, so
each netlayer answers that for itself: `tcp` stays cleartext to match Goblins byte
for byte, while `iroh` is encrypted because its QUIC channel is keyed and `webrtc`
because its DataChannel runs DTLS. A confidential TCP (TLS over a socket) would be
another netlayer under the same contract with its own transport symbol; nothing
forbids it, and it is not written yet. Where pancake heads beyond today's
transports lives in the
[vision](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/vision.md).
